WEBVTT

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.000
You can add even more complexity to your conditional statements by asking two

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.080
questions at the same time.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:17.800
We use the AND and OR operators to check two Boolean expressions at once.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.960
Let's talk about OR first.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:24.880
If we wanted to, for example, express that you can be younger than five years old or

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:28.800
older than 95 years old to get free admission to a museum,

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.800
we could express that like this.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:41.400
Expressions that use the OR operator are true if either the first or

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.720
the second expression is true.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:50.520
Only when both expressions are false does the OR expression evaluate to false.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.080
The AND operator is more strict.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:56.760
What if we wanted to say that to get the student pricing at the museum,

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.000
you need to be between 12 and 21 years old?

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.760
We could express that as checking whether age is greater than 12 and

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:06.840
age is less than 21.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:10.600
If only one of these conditions is true, that's not good enough.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:13.400
Both conditions must be true to get the student discount.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:20.000
More and more complex questions can be represented with combinations of AND and

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.520
OR statements.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:25.480
What if the museum gives the student pricing to everyone if it's Tuesday or

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:26.560
Thursday?

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:30.200
But on every other day, you still need to be between the ages of 12 and

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.480
21 to get a discount.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:34.680
We could express that like this.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:39.600
If the day is Tuesday or the day is Thursday, or

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.120
your age is greater than 12 and age is less than 21,

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.760
then you get the student pricing.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:49.880
Else, you get regular pricing.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:54.520
When using text, use parentheses to group Boolean expressions together.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:56.800
When evaluating this complex conditional,

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.600
the computer will evaluate each Boolean expression separately and

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:05.080
then use the rules of AND and OR to determine if the whole expression is true

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:06.600
or false.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.840
So if the day is Monday and the user is 25, the code would run like this.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.480
First, the computer checks if the day is Tuesday.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:17.920
It's not, so that is false.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.920
Then the computer checks if the day is Thursday.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:24.000
It's also not, so that is false as well.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.320
Since it's not Tuesday or Thursday, and at least one side must be true for

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.520
an OR statement to evaluate to true, that whole expression evaluates to false.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:38.960
Then the computer checks the other side of the OR, since that could be true.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:42.160
The computer checks if the age is greater than 12.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:46.000
25 is greater than 12, so that statement is true.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.280
Now the computer checks if the age is less than 21.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:53.880
25 is not less than 21, so that statement is false.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:57.880
Since this was an AND statement, but only one side was true,

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:00.640
this whole expression evaluates to false.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:03.680
And the message displays that the user gets regular pricing.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:07.960
For a human, perhaps this answer was obvious from the beginning,

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:11.040
because we are actually pretty good at dealing with logic statements

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.640
when they represent real life decision making.

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:17.800
But the computer needs to evaluate every expression until it can determine

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.640
whether the condition is true or false.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:25.360
Many beginner programmers make a mistake while trying to write this expression.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:27.080
Is the day Tuesday or Thursday?

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:32.800
Notice the subtle difference between the correct code and this version.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:35.560
This is often the result of the fact that in English,

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:38.840
we would say, is the day Tuesday or Thursday?

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:42.080
However, remember that computer questions are different.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:45.240
So we have to ask, is the day equal to Tuesday?

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.520
Or is the day equal to Thursday?

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.200
So that the computer will understand.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:53.520
Computers do exactly what we program them to do.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:57.560
But with conditionals, we can simulate complex decision making and

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:01.720
have the computer adapt to changes, or even seem intelligent to humans.
